  "key_findings": [
    "K=1 scaling is near-linear with candidate count (R² ≈ 1.0)",
    "K=2 requires 15.9x more candidates than K=1 5g (667 vs 42), takes 7.6x more wall-clock time (59.0s vs 7.8s) due to batched GPU efficiency",
    "Per-candidate time decreases at higher candidate counts (0.38ms→0.18ms) due to amortized kernel launch overhead",
    "K=1 robustness: 86.2% (5g) — attacker flips any single categorical group",
    "K=2 robustness: 61.6% (5g) — attacker flips any pair of categorical groups",
    "Adding is_sm_ips_ports (|G|=2) and is_ftp_login (|G|=4) to K=1 adds only 6 candidates (38→42) and 0.6s — marginal cost",
    "The state group (|G|=11) is the dominant cost driver in K=1 (25→36, +11 candidates)"
